00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:08.430
It's not really affecting the overall door-To-Door
travel time that much.

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:13.019
And so the number of people who are prepared
to pay, you know, that much more money to

00:13:13.019 --> 00:13:18.139
just take a take three or four hours off the
middle of a 12 hour process was not a big

00:13:18.139 --> 00:13:19.139
enough to make it work.

00:13:19.139 --> 00:13:22.310
And we'll see what happens with this new generation
of supersonic planes.

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:25.629
But I mean, the I you can't fly supersonic
over land.

00:13:25.629 --> 00:13:28.560
So they're trying to come up with various
fixes for that.

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:32.480
But if there's one route to the world it was
going to work on, it would be London, JFK.

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:36.899
And that was the route that Concorde was most
effective, although it didn't add up.

00:13:36.899 --> 00:13:37.899
So.

00:13:37.899 --> 00:13:40.629
So, yeah, I think the thing to watch out for
here and I don't go into this in the book

00:13:40.629 --> 00:13:45.999
because it's it's not about wheels, but it's
this idea of suborbital transport.

00:13:45.999 --> 00:13:48.740
And this is something that Elon Musk has talked
about.

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:51.480
I mean, people have talked about this for
ages, but he's actually building a vehicle

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:56.629
now that that may even fly the next few weeks
that we might see doing this.

00:13:56.629 --> 00:13:58.509
And this is where you basically launch a rocket.

00:13:58.509 --> 00:14:02.410
But instead of going into orbit to start to
get if you go into orbit, the space station

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:03.879
orbits about once every 90 minutes.

00:14:03.879 --> 00:14:08.329
If you get into the lowest possible Earth
orbit, you're going around the world once

00:14:08.329 --> 00:14:09.329
every 90 minutes.

00:14:09.329 --> 00:14:12.509
And another way of looking that that is if
you lock yourself into an orbit that doesn't

00:14:12.509 --> 00:14:15.269
quite it doesn't quite make it into orbit.

00:14:15.269 --> 00:14:18.490
So it's just suborbital and then comes down
on the other side of the world.

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:21.470
You'll get there in 45 minutes to an hour.

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:24.649
So this is the idea that you could fly from
pretty much anywhere to anywhere in less than

00:14:24.649 --> 00:14:27.230
an hour if you took a suborbital trajectory.

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:30.690
And the starship may be able to do that, then
you've got this big problem again, if you

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:35.170
have to build space ports outside cities and
Moscow is talking about floating space ports.

00:14:35.170 --> 00:14:41.860
And so so that's that's a potential game changer
in terms of those things.

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:44.600
But I think we're still going to come back
to you know, I like to say, even if we build

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:48.430
hyperspace tunnels from London to New York,
you're still going to have to have security

00:14:48.430 --> 00:14:49.430
around them.

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:52.089
You're still going to have limited capacity
and you're going to have to go to the hyperspace

00:14:52.089 --> 00:14:54.320
port, which is probably on the other side
of town.

00:14:54.320 --> 00:15:01.279
So then you then your London to JFK, you know,
maybe you can maybe you can take a few hours

00:15:01.279 --> 00:15:04.050
out of it again, but you're still looking
to get down to zero and you can have that

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:08.589
technology at everyone's homes and you could,
like, step through your hyperspace port into

00:15:08.589 --> 00:15:13.010
your holiday home in another country that
I can't see that that can't see that working.

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:14.450
And we don't even know if this is possible.

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:18.110
So so, yeah, I think it's a very good point
that actually these boring logistical constraints

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:20.519
actually end up being the most important factor.

00:15:20.519 --> 00:15:25.259
Speaker 1: We've been speaking with the book's
author, Tom Standage, who's deputy editor

00:15:25.259 --> 00:15:26.259
of The Economist.

00:15:26.259 --> 00:15:28.230
Tom, really a pleasure speaking with you today.

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:29.230
I appreciate it.

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:30.230
Speaker 5: Thank you.

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:30.439
Thanks for having me.

